TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"2.2 Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Appendix A: STK files and settings"},{"id":"R11","met":"yes","where":"Contents"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The proposed 50 Mbit/s X-band downlink is not substantiated by a link budget. The report acknowledges this; therefore the claimed daily capacity and margin are preliminary rather than demonstrated."},{"where":"2.3 Orbit","issue":"The five-day reach estimate divides equatorial ground-track spacing by the maximum roll-reach width. This is an idealized geometric estimate; it does not establish actual five-day coverage of every reef location once imaging geometry, pointing limits, sunlight, clouds and acquisition scheduling are considered."},{"where":"Appendix A: STK files and settings","issue":"The report says the STK files are submitted in a ZIP, but the files themselves are not present in the supplied submission text, so their completeness and reproducibility cannot be verified."}],"overall":"A strong, well-structured preliminary design with a clear Australian mission purpose, substantial orbit and access analysis, and useful STK imagery. The principal weaknesses are unverified communications performance and idealized coverage assumptions; the claimed software files are also not verifiable in the supplied material.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":9.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Clearly explains the mission need and rationale, specifies a single spacecraft and why it is sufficient, and identifies the operator and several customers. The environmental context and intended public benefit are well described and supported with citations. A little more qualification of whether 5 m multispectral imagery can reliably identify bleaching would strengthen the case.","evidence":[{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"The primary mission of ZYZZsat is to image the whole Great Barrier Reef in visible bands at 5 m resolution"},{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"One satellite is therefore used."},{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"The operator is the Australian Institute of Marine Science (AIMS)"},{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"The customers are the Great Barrier Reef Marine Park Authority"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":48.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"The design covers launch selection and fallback, orbit elements and J2 sun-synchronous calculation, manoeuvres, STK access analysis, contact frequency, data budget, mission duration and disposal. Screenshots and hand calculations provide useful evidence. The analysis is substantial but not fully validated: the assumed 50 Mbit/s link lacks a link budget, imager performance and operational constraints are lightly treated, and the one-station operations case is an assumption.","evidence":[{"where":"2.2 Launch","quote":"The baseline is an SSMS rideshare flight on Vega C from Kourou in early 2025"},{"where":"2.3 Orbit","quote":"The orbit is circular, so it has no perigee"},{"where":"2.3 Orbit","quote":"No orbital manoeuvres are required."},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","quote":"There are six passes each day, three in the evening and three in the morning (UTC)"},{"where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"The mission lasts five years."},{"where":"Appendix A: STK files and settings","quote":"The following STK 11 files are submitted with this report in ZYZZsat_STK.zip."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion draws specific findings from the design and openly identifies consequential limitations, including launch uncertainty, unproven fluid-lensing benefit, missing link budget and revisit limitations. It could more explicitly distinguish demonstrated performance from preliminary estimates, but it is appropriately evidence-based.","evidence":[{"where":"3 Conclusion","quote":"The design has four limitations."},{"where":"3 Conclusion","quote":"With these points addressed in the next design phase, the mission can provide the direct, regular bleaching maps"}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":16.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a logical structure, executive summary, numbered sections, labelled tables and figures, and generally clear professional writing. References are cited throughout and a bibliography is provided. Some source entries are incomplete or less authoritative than ideal, and the many dense calculations and tables could be more consistently supported with assumptions and validation. The STK appendix lists files, but their contents cannot be assessed from the report text alone.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report presents the design of ZYZZsat"},{"where":"Contents","quote":"3 Conclusion"},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","quote":"Table 5: STK access report, ZYZZsat to Mount Pleasant Radio Observatory (elevation above 0°)."},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[11] D. A. Vallado, Fundamentals of Astrodynamics and Applications"}]}]}
